Este prompt está diseñado para que Antigravity haga una mejora exclusivamente visual, sin modificar ninguna funcionalidad, cálculo, contenido clínico ni navegación.

Improve the visual design, consistency, and responsive presentation of the existing Med-Peptides product monograph page.

This is a UI and UX refinement only. Do not change, remove, add, or reinterpret any existing functionality.

Primary objective

Create a more cohesive, professional, and clinically credible interface inspired by Google Cloud Console and Material Design 3 principles:

* Clear information hierarchy
* Consistent component structure
* Predictable spacing
* Restrained use of color
* Strong alignment
* Accessible contrast
* Unified iconography
* Responsive tables and content
* Minimal decorative effects
* High information density without visual clutter

The final result should feel like one unified pharmaceutical platform, rather than several independently designed modules placed on the same page.

Strict functional constraints

Do not change:

* Existing calculations
* Reconstitution logic
* Dose-selection logic
* Clinical phases
* Syringe calibration behavior
* Sliders, selectors, buttons, links, downloads, or QR codes
* Product or batch data
* Tables or table content
* Warnings, disclaimers, or regulatory text
* Navigation
* User roles or permissions
* Data sources or API calls
* Existing routes
* Any business or clinical rule

Do not add new functionality.

Do not remove any information.

Only improve layout, styling, visual hierarchy, component consistency, responsiveness, and accessibility.

1. Standardize all first-level sections

Every first-level content section must begin with the same navy-blue section header.

Apply this consistently to:

* Analytical Matrix and Clinical Specifications
* Reconstitution Protocol and Dosimetry
* Batch Traceability and Analytical Assurance
* Physical Vial Labels and Batch Printing
* Any other equivalent first-level module

Use one reusable SectionHeader component.

All first-level headers must have:

* The same background color
* The same minimum height
* The same internal padding
* The same border radius
* The same title position
* The same icon container
* The same title typography
* The same subtitle typography
* The same position for badges and status indicators
* The same responsive behavior

Recommended structure:

1. Icon
2. Section title
3. Short descriptive subtitle
4. Optional status badge aligned consistently
5. Optional secondary metadata or category label

Use a deep navy color close to #003B70 or the current primary navy.

Avoid creating different header layouts for different sections.

1. Equalize section widths and spacing

All first-level sections must occupy the same available width and align to the same page grid.

Use:

* One consistent maximum content width
* One consistent horizontal page padding
* The same distance between sections
* The same internal section padding
* The same corner radius
* The same border treatment
* The same subtle elevation level

No section should appear narrower, wider, more indented, or visually heavier than another section at the same hierarchy level.

Use an 8-pixel spacing system:

* 4 px for very small internal separation
* 8 px between tightly related elements
* 16 px between controls
* 24 px between component groups
* 32 px between major blocks
* 48 px between first-level sections where appropriate

Avoid arbitrary spacing values.

1. Establish a consistent page hierarchy

Use the following hierarchy throughout the page:

Level 1

Full-width module with navy header.

Level 2

Subsection within the module, using a simple title, small supporting icon, and optional divider.

Level 3

Cards, selectors, tables, metrics, notices, and instructional content.

Do not style Level 2 or Level 3 elements as if they were new first-level sections.

Avoid having several competing visual headers inside the same module.

The user should be able to understand the page structure immediately from scrolling alone.

1. Redesign the syringe visualization

Keep the existing syringe behavior, values, calibration, calculations, markings, and warning logic exactly as they are.

Only redesign its visual presentation.

The syringe currently looks like a separate neon-style graphic. Integrate it with the rest of the clinical interface.

Use:

* The same navy, blue, cyan, gray, and restrained green palette used elsewhere
* Flat or lightly elevated Material-style surfaces
* Subtle borders
* Controlled gradients only where necessary to communicate liquid volume
* The same typography as the rest of the interface
* The same icon style
* The same corner radius as other clinical cards

Remove or significantly reduce:

* Neon glow
* Strong red illumination
* Excessive dark contrast
* Decorative effects that make the syringe resemble a gaming interface
* Heavy shadows
* Visual treatments not used elsewhere on the page

The syringe barrel should remain clearly distinguishable from the background, but it should look like a precise clinical calibration diagram.

Recommended visual treatment:

* Neutral light or navy container consistent with the surrounding section
* White or very light syringe barrel
* Blue or cyan liquid fill
* Dark navy measurement markings
* Clear selected draw line
* Small, restrained warning color only when a genuine warning is active
* Consistent labels for units, milliliters, and maximum syringe capacity

If the calculated dose exceeds one U-100 syringe, represent this warning clearly without making the entire visualization red.

1. Normalize card design

Create a common card system for:

* Product specifications
* Vial and solvent selection
* Clinical phases
* Calibration results
* Analytical metrics
* Protocol steps
* Label-download options
* Regulatory notices

Use no more than three card variants:

1. Standard information card
2. Selected or active card
3. Warning or regulatory card

All standard cards should share:

* Border radius
* Border color
* Internal padding
* Typography
* Shadow or elevation
* Background color
* Hover behavior where applicable

Selected cards should use:

* Primary blue border
* Light blue tonal background
* Clear selected indicator
* No excessive glow or heavy shadow

Warning cards should use amber only for real warnings.

Green should be reserved for:

* Verified
* Passed
* Available
* Authenticated
* Successful status

Purple should be used only when it communicates a distinct, consistent category. Do not use decorative colors without semantic meaning.

1. Improve clinical phase cards

Preserve every clinical phase and all associated values and selection behavior.

Correct the current visual collisions between:

* “Phase” labels
* Dose badges
* Phase numbers
* Active-state labels
* Titles and values

Create a stable card layout:

* Phase number and dose badge in the top row
* Phase name underneath
* Primary dose value in a clearly defined position
* State text such as “Select” or “Active phase” at the bottom
* Equal card width and height
* Consistent internal padding

Text must never overlap at any breakpoint.

Active phase styling should remain clearly visible but use the same selected-state language as all other selected controls.

1. Improve typography

Use a typography system aligned with Google Material principles.

Recommended hierarchy:

* Page title: 32–36 px
* First-level section title: 18–20 px
* Section subtitle: 14–16 px
* Level 2 heading: 16–18 px
* Card title: 14–16 px
* Body text: 14–16 px
* Supporting text: 13–14 px
* Labels and metadata: 12–13 px

Use consistent line heights and font weights.

Avoid excessive uppercase text. Uppercase may remain for short technical labels, but long headings should use sentence case.

Reduce letter spacing where the current uppercase headings feel overly expanded.

Use monospace only for:

* Batch codes
* Molecular sequences
* Technical identifiers
* Values that require exact character differentiation

1. Standardize icons

Replace inconsistent emoji-style graphics with a unified icon family.

Use Material Symbols or an equivalent clean outlined icon system.

Apply the same:

* Stroke weight
* Optical size
* Color
* Container size
* Alignment

This applies especially to:

* Vial presentation
* Syringe and reconstitution
* Batch verification
* Laboratory analysis
* QR and traceability
* Shipping labels
* Client vial labels
* Download actions
* Clinical notices

Do not mix emojis, filled icons, outlined icons, and decorative illustrations in the same hierarchy.

Icons must support recognition, not act as decoration.

1. Refine colors and surfaces

Retain the existing pharmaceutical blue identity, but simplify the palette.

Recommended semantic structure:

* Primary navy: section headers and primary emphasis
* Primary blue: active controls, links, and selected borders
* Cyan: secondary technical emphasis
* Green: verification and successful quality status
* Amber: warnings requiring attention
* Red: critical errors only
* Neutral gray: secondary text, dividers, and inactive controls
* Light blue-gray: page and card backgrounds

Avoid using multiple unrelated greens, blues, purples, and cyan tones for components of the same type.

Use tonal surfaces instead of heavy shadows.

Use one elevation treatment for standard sections and another subtle elevation treatment for interactive elements.

 1. Improve tables

Preserve all table columns, rows, values, and behavior.

Improve:

* Header contrast
* Cell padding
* Column alignment
* Row height
* Selected-row treatment
* Numeric alignment
* Responsive behavior
* Horizontal scrolling

Tables must not be clipped on the right side.

On narrower screens:

* Place the table inside a clearly scrollable horizontal container
* Keep column headers visible
* Preserve the selected-row indicator
* Do not truncate essential clinical values
* Do not silently hide columns
* Provide a subtle visual cue that horizontal scrolling is available

Use tabular numerals for concentrations, doses, volumes, and analytical results.

 1. Align controls and selection patterns

Use one visual language for all selectable controls:

* Dose buttons
* Vial strength buttons
* BAC water volume buttons
* Clinical phase cards
* Unit selectors
* Presentation selectors

Every control must have the same clearly defined states:

* Default
* Hover
* Focus
* Selected
* Disabled

Selected controls should not use different colors or interaction patterns in different sections.

Ensure all interactive targets are at least 44 × 44 px where practical.

 1. Improve notices and regulatory content

Preserve the complete regulatory wording.

Create consistent styles for:

* Informational notice
* Clinical warning
* Mandatory prescriber directive
* Verified quality notice
* Technical reference note

Use a small semantic icon, a concise heading, and readable body text.

Avoid nested boxes inside boxes unless they communicate a necessary hierarchy.

The “Clinical Pharmacopoeial Directive and Prescriber Responsibility Notice” should remain visually important, but its appearance must be consistent with the rest of the platform.

 1. Label and document section

Bring “Physical Vial Labels and Batch Printing” into the same first-level module system.

Add the standard navy header at the beginning of this section.

Keep both label options functionally unchanged.

Make the two label cards:

* Equal width
* Equal height
* Structurally identical
* Aligned by title, description, primary button, and secondary actions

Use semantic color only to differentiate the shipping label from the client vial label.

The document preview should use the same container width, radius, border, and spacing as the other major content blocks.

 1. Responsive behavior

Optimize the page for desktop, tablet, and mobile without modifying functionality.

Desktop

* Use a stable centered content grid
* Align all sections to the same width
* Keep related controls in balanced two-column layouts
* Avoid excessive empty space

Tablet

* Reduce gutters consistently
* Allow two-column areas to stack when content becomes compressed
* Preserve control sizes and readable typography

Mobile

* Stack cards vertically
* Make action buttons full-width where appropriate
* Allow tables to scroll horizontally
* Keep section headers compact but consistent
* Place badges below titles when necessary
* Prevent all text, badge, and control overlap
* Ensure the syringe remains legible without horizontal clipping

 1. Accessibility

Follow Material Design and WCAG-oriented accessibility principles.

Ensure:

* Minimum 4.5:1 contrast for normal text
* Minimum 3:1 contrast for large text and meaningful graphical elements
* Visible keyboard focus states
* Color is never the only indicator of status
* Icons have accessible labels
* Tables retain correct semantic structure
* Buttons and links remain distinguishable
* Selected states use more than color alone
* Text remains readable at 200% zoom
* No content overlaps when browser text size is increased
* Motion is minimal and respects reduced-motion preferences

 1. Visual cleanup

Remove unnecessary visual noise:

* Excessive nested borders
* Inconsistent shadows
* Repeated labels that do not add meaning
* Unnecessary decorative badges
* Mixed icon styles
* Uneven card heights
* Misaligned controls
* Large empty gaps
* Inconsistent section margins
* Text clipping and overlap
* Unnecessary gradients and glow effects

Do not make the interface visually sterile. Preserve the scientific and pharmaceutical identity while making the page calmer, more coherent, and easier to scan.

 1. Implementation approach

First identify and reuse shared components and design tokens.

Create or consolidate tokens for:

* Colors
* Spacing
* Typography
* Border radius
* Borders
* Elevation
* Section width
* Header height
* Control height
* Icon size
* Focus ring

Prefer reusable components such as:

* SectionHeader
* ContentSection
* InformationCard
* MetricCard
* StatusBadge
* SelectionButton
* ClinicalNotice
* ResponsiveDataTable
* SectionIcon
* SyringeVisualization

Do not duplicate styles for individual sections.

Acceptance criteria

The redesign is complete only when:

* Every first-level section begins with the same navy header pattern
* Every first-level section occupies the same page width
* Section spacing is consistent
* Equivalent cards have equal dimensions
* The syringe matches the visual language of the rest of the application
* No clinical phase text overlaps
* No table is clipped
* All existing functionality works exactly as before
* No data, calculations, copy, routes, or interactions have changed
* The layout works on desktop, tablet, and mobile
* Keyboard focus is visible
* Color contrast is accessible
* The result looks like a unified Google-quality clinical platform

Before finishing, compare the updated interface against the current screenshots and verify that all changes are visual only.
